{"packet_id":"c8f858653fccb769","dimensions":{"problem_framing":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A1"],"reason":"A1 separates the objective from the suggested solution and adds a licensing alternative, which would merit 2. However, it introduces a €20,000 budget and a 12-week timeline that appear nowhere in the case or sources. It also lists shipping and aircraft as existing B2B products, while acr-mr-military mentions only military and commercial users. The invented numbers cap this dimension at 1."},"causal_mechanism":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"A9 states who changes behaviour, why, and how ACR captures value, so a chain is present. Two links are unsupported. First, the behaviour-change link (users switch from phone or radio) misreads acr-fr-purpose, which says the PLB is for use after those means have been exhausted. Second, value capture rests on an 'established safety reputation' that no source shows."},"distinct_theories":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 and T2 share the same customer type, direct-sales channel, premium pricing and reputation lever, and differ only in the stated motive (reliability versus convenience). They are close rewordings of one idea rather than rivals implying different products, customers or channels. The licensing and B2B alternatives from A1 never became theories."},"assumptions_predictions":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"Predictions are observable: survey intent at a premium and a pilot conversion rate. The competitor-share prediction is vague. The thresholds are arbitrary assumptions, and T2's own premium assumption conflicts with how T2 is later tested in A16, which weakens how far failed predictions would really damage the theory."},"evidence_judgement":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A11","A12","A14","A15"],"reason":"Each assessment states a 'does not show' limit, but several judgements are poor. Federal Register and news excerpts are labelled independent_study, although they are verified observations. A11 counts a regulatory statement of purpose as support for T1 even though it implies the device is a last resort. A15 treats acr-gcn-three as neutral when it shows two approved competitors, which bears directly on differentiation. The mandatory NOAA registration in acr-fr-registration, a possible purchase friction, is never assessed. No source's date, place or fit to the customer group is discussed."},"seeking_challenge":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A13"],"reason":"A13 runs a single challenge search against T1 but never reports or assesses what it found. The available competitor evidence (acr-gcn-three) and the last-resort framing (acr-fr-purpose) were not used against T1."},"experiment_design":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A16"],"reason":"Both theories predict at least 60% purchase intent; they are separated only by WTP of at least €150 versus at most €120. That split contradicts T2's own premise of a willingness to pay a premium (A10), so the test does not cleanly separate the rivals. The '€150 = 20% premium' figure implies an invented standard PLB price. The decision rule leaves gaps (40–60% intent), and the 150/150 gender quota is unjustified."},"revision":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A18"],"reason":"A18 correctly identifies SYN-D as simulated and irrelevant to beacon demand, and justifiably retains T1. It does not explicitly trace the implication for the recommendation."},"recommendation":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A17","A19"],"reason":"A19 says 'invest' while its next action is to validate demand first, which is inconsistent. It rates T1 'likely' with no demand evidence. It markets the product as 'the only device that can reach rescue satellites', which acr-gcn-three contradicts. It asserts 'NOAA-certified', which the sources do not show (they show NOAA registration and RTCM standards). The text is also truncated ('if.', 'using 4.'). Reversal conditions are present but do not rescue the score."}},"red_flags":[{"kind":"invented_observation","event_ids":["A1","A16"],"reason":"A1 introduces a €20,000/12-week budget and shipping/aircraft product lines not found in the dossier. A16's '€150 = 20% premium over standard PLB' implies an unsourced standard price."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A9","A10","A19"],"reason":"Three claims are asserted without support. (1) ACR's 'established safety reputation', stated as fact. (2) NOAA certification, whereas the sources show NOAA registration and RTCM standards. (3) The beacon as 'the only device' reaching rescue satellites, contradicted by acr-gcn-three listing two other FCC-approved PLBs."},{"kind":"misleading_quote","event_ids":["A9","A10","A11"],"reason":"acr-fr-purpose says the PLB is for use after phone or radio have been exhausted. The theories instead claim users switch from those tools or no longer need to carry them, and A11 cites the excerpt as support for T1."},{"kind":"non_discriminating_test","event_ids":["A16"],"reason":"Both theories predict the same purchase intent. The WTP split assigned to T2 contradicts T2's own premium premise, so the test outcome cannot cleanly adjudicate between the theories."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A11","A14","A15"],"reason":"Verified regulatory and news observations are misclassified as independent studies."}],"suitable_for_training":false,"screen_reason":"The episode is complete, but it contains several critical errors. These include invented numbers and constraints, and a misread source repurposed as support. The recommendation makes an 'only device' claim contradicted by the dossier's competitor evidence, and the proposed test cannot cleanly separate T1 from T2. Any one of these errors would be enough to reject the trajectory."}
